# The example scripts and the stamps of their pictures are hashed
# (`cargo xtask example-thumbs`, xtask/src/thumbs.rs): LF in every
# checkout, also with core.autocrlf on Windows.
docs/examples/*.json text eol=lf
crates/engine/tests/example_models/*.json text eol=lf
xtask/example-pictures.txt text eol=lf
# Files tests read byte for byte, and files the packages carry as they are
# (a desktop entry, a shell script and AppRun must keep LF on Linux and
# macOS even when they were checked out on Windows): LF in every checkout.
# On Windows with core.autocrlf the golden STEP text and the desktop entry
# came out with CRLF, and their tests failed (CI, 2026-10-30).
crates/*/tests/golden/** text eol=lf
# The guide: tests take its examples and the output it prints from the
# text (a cut list's CSV, docs/guide/files.md); with CRLF on Windows the
# fences were not found (CI, 2026-11-09).
docs/guide/*.md text eol=lf
# The zip reader's test data: deflate streams and the text they inflate
# to, compared byte for byte (`include_bytes!`, crates/zipstore/src/
# tests.rs); git took two of them for text, which CRLF would change.
crates/zipstore/testdata/** -text
packaging/** text eol=lf
*.step text eol=lf
# The cargo configuration and the workspace's manifest, whose lines xtask
# tests read (the static C runtime for Windows, the repository the AppImage
# updates from): LF in every checkout. The tests read them line by line as
# well, so a checkout made before this rule passes too (2026-11-12).
/.cargo/config.toml text eol=lf
/Cargo.toml text eol=lf
# Binary files last, so no text rule above (packaging/**) claims them:
# a later line wins in .gitattributes.
*.png binary
*.hdr binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.3dcraft binary
*.3dcraftjig binary
*.3dcraftasm binary
